---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import About from "../components/About.astro";
import sweepFig from "../figures/taiwan_sweep.png";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
const sections = [
  { id: "the-fault-and-the-mesh", label: "The fault and the mesh" },
  { id: "the-tensors", label: "The tensors" },
  { id: "what-is-solved", label: "What is solved" },
  { id: "what-the-sweep-says", label: "What the sweep says" },
  { id: "what-this-is-not", label: "What this is not" },
  { id: "reproducing-it", label: "Reproducing it" },
];
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Taiwan"
  description="The Longitudinal Valley Fault of eastern Taiwan, from two Taiwan Earthquake Model segments, under a sweep of regional stress tensors: the static Coulomb LCP's slip, activation load and rake on every element, for the mollified half-space kernel and the classical dislocation, two effective-stress models, and loads to six times the first failure."
  current="taiwan"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Taiwan<span class="sub">the Longitudinal Valley Fault under a sweep of stress tensors</span></h1>
    <p>
      One real fault instead of a synthetic one. Two Taiwan Earthquake Model
      segments of the Longitudinal Valley Fault, 2,498 triangles striking
      N20E and dipping about 60&deg; east to 20&nbsp;km, breaking the surface
      along 147&nbsp;km of trace, loaded by a regional stress whose shape and
      orientation are swept: the most compressive axis at seven azimuths
      from 90&deg; to 150&deg;, and a regime from pure thrust to pure
      strike-slip in five steps.
    </p>
    <p>
      At each tensor the static Coulomb problem is solved along the load,
      from the first failure to six times it, under two slip rules: a fixed
      rake, each element slipping along the shear the tensor resolves on it,
      and a free rake, each element&rsquo;s slip a vector that turns to
      follow the total shear. What is painted is one value per element: the
      slip at a chosen load, the load at which the element first slipped, the
      rake of that slip, the rake the tensor imposes, or how fast the tensor
      loads the element toward failure. Two effective-normal-stress models
      and three kernels, one of them symmetric and positive definite, so
      that neither the strength profile nor the kernel can be mistaken for
      the result, and the uniqueness of the answer is a theorem on at
      least one of them.
    </p>
    <nav class="toc">
      <h3>Contents</h3>
      <ol>{sections.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>
    <hr />
    <p class="contact">
      Produced by <code>studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py</code> and gated by
      <code>selfcheck.py -k taiwan</code>. The segments are the user&rsquo;s
      meshes of the Taiwan Earthquake Model seismogenic structures, used as
      supplied.
    </p>
    <About />
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="onfault">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="rake"><span>slip rule</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="theta"><span>S<sub>Hmax</sub> azimuth</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="r"><span>regime r</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="model"><span>effective stress</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="metric"><span>metric</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="load"><span>load</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="window"><span class="lbl">window</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="The two segments of the Longitudinal Valley Fault, coloured by the static LCP's slip, activation load, rake or Coulomb loading rate, one value per element"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mark"><span>mark saturated</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="wire"><span>element edges</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-fault-and-the-mesh">The fault and the mesh</h2>
  <p>
    The Longitudinal Valley Fault is the suture between the Philippine Sea
    plate and Eurasia in eastern Taiwan, an east-dipping reverse fault with
    a left-lateral component that creeps along part of its length and
    broke in 1951 and 2003. The two segments here are its southern 88&nbsp;km
    and northern 58&nbsp;km, meeting at one trace vertex, which is the origin
    of the local frame: x east, y north, z up, kilometres, the surface at
    z&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. Geographic azimuths are clockwise from north, so the
    fault&rsquo;s normal points to about 110&deg;.
  </p>
  <p>
    The meshes are used as supplied: 1,499 and 999 triangles with a median
    edge of 1.9&nbsp;km, a smallest edge of 0.12&nbsp;km and two near-vertical
    slivers at the bottom and the top. Every triangle is wound so that its
    normal points up, cutde&rsquo;s frame is checked against the
    package&rsquo;s on every facet, the tiling is checked to be a manifold
    with one boundary loop per segment, and the reader is checked against
    meshio bit for bit. The mollification width is a tenth of the median
    edge, 0.19&nbsp;km, and the free surface is folded into the kernel as on
    the <a href={`${base}onfault/`}>on-fault page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-tensors">The tensors</h2>
  <p>
    The load is a unit deviator times &lambda;, the maximum shear stress the
    regional field carries, plus an isotropic part of two thirds of &lambda;,
    on an effective normal stress of 150&nbsp;MPa everywhere or lithostatic
    minus a pore-pressure ratio of 0.4. The deviator&rsquo;s most compressive
    axis is horizontal at azimuth &theta;, and its shape is set by a regime
    parameter r: at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0 the vertical stress is the least
    compressive (the thrust regime), at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;1 it is the
    intermediate one (strike-slip), and in between the principal values are
    (&minus;1, r, 1&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;r) scaled to unit maximum shear, so that
    &lambda; means the same thing on the whole family and
    r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5 is the axially symmetric compression where the two
    regimes are equally favoured. Friction is 0.6 everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tensor also resolves a shear direction on each element, and that
    rake is the map labelled <i>rake the tensor imposes</i>: with the
    compressive axis at 110&deg;, normal to the fault, every element is
    loaded reverse; at 90&deg; and 100&deg; a right-lateral component
    appears, and from 120&deg; on a left-lateral one, which is the sense
    observed on the fault. Under the fixed rule that is the rake the slip
    has; under the free rule the slip may turn away from it, and the map
    labelled <i>rake of the slip</i> is what the solve chose.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-solved">What is solved</h2>
  <p>
    Two slip rules on the same matrix. The <b>fixed rake</b> is the static
    LCP of the <a href={`${base}avalanche/`}>avalanche page</a>: one
    strength per element, slip along the tensor&rsquo;s shear wherever that
    shear reaches the strength and nowhere else, solved by projected
    Gauss&ndash;Seidel. The <b>free rake</b> is the same problem with the
    constraint removed: each element&rsquo;s slip is a vector in its plane,
    Coulomb friction is a disc rather than a threshold, an element is locked
    strictly inside the disc or slips on its rim parallel to the total shear
    it carries, regional plus what every other element&rsquo;s slip adds.
    It is solved by the contact-dynamics form of the same sweep, a
    projection onto the disc per element, and it reduces to the fixed rule
    exactly when the slip is confined to the tensor&rsquo;s direction. The
    two differ where the neighbours turn an element&rsquo;s shear off the
    regional rake, and where the fixed rule&rsquo;s non-negativity forbids
    an element from slipping against it. No events. The load is scanned from
    &lambda;<sub>0</sub>, the load at which the best-oriented element reaches
    its strength with no interaction under the uniform model, to
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>, warm-started, and the lithostatic model is
    scanned at the same absolute loads, so the two models are compared under
    one tectonic stress. The quantities quoted below are in MPa of maximum
    shear, which is what makes tensors of different shape comparable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things the linear Coulomb criterion cannot say are stated beside
    the maps instead. An element whose normal stress the tensor raises faster
    than its shear can never fail at any load, and the legend gives that area
    for the chosen tensor. And an element whose effective normal stress the
    load drives through zero would open rather than slip; the manifest
    records the area in that state at every load; it stays below 1.2&nbsp;%
    of the fault on either model within the scan.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Three operators, one of them a theorem.</b> The collocation kernels,
    mollified and classical, read each element&rsquo;s traction at its
    centroid, and the matrix that results is not symmetric. The fixed-rake
    problem still has exactly one solution whenever the symmetric part of
    the area-weighted operator is positive definite (that makes it a
    P-matrix), and on this mesh it is, for every kernel and every tensor;
    but for a non-symmetric matrix that is a fact about this mesh, checked
    by an eigenvalue after the fact, and the convergence of the sweeps
    stays a measurement. The third kernel is the mollified one in its
    <b>Galerkin</b> form: each row is the traction averaged over the
    receiver element with nine Gauss points, test and trial space both the
    piecewise constant, which by reciprocity makes the area-weighted matrix
    symmetric up to quadrature error, and it is then symmetrised exactly.
    With that operator symmetric and positive definite the fixed-rake
    problem is a strictly convex quadratic programme, the free-rake problem
    a strongly convex minimisation, each with one solution, and the
    projected Gauss&ndash;Seidel sweeps converge by theorem. The price is
    a few per cent of accuracy at the same mollification width (2&nbsp;%
    softer than Eshelby on the gate&rsquo;s penny, where collocation is
    1&nbsp;% stiff) and ten times the assembly: 29&nbsp;minutes against 3
    on this mesh with eight workers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-the-sweep-says">What the sweep says</h2>
  <figure class="panels">
    <img src={sweepFig.src} alt="Eight panels: first-failure load, half-active load for both stress models, free-over-fixed moment, area-mean rake, never-failing area, activation curves at 130 degrees, and the rake rotation" />
    <figcaption>
      <b>The sweep in eight numbers.</b> <b>a</b> the first-failure load
      &lambda;<sub>0</sub> against the azimuth of the most compressive axis,
      one line per regime. <b>b</b>, <b>c</b> the load at which half the
      fault&rsquo;s area is slipping, uniform and lithostatic effective
      stress, solid for the free rake and dashed for the fixed one, both on
      the mollified kernel. <b>d</b> the moment under the free rake over the
      fixed one at 3&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>. <b>e</b> the area-mean rake
      the tensor imposes. <b>f</b> the area the tensor can never bring to
      failure. <b>g</b> the active area against the load at 130&deg;, free
      rake, solid uniform, dotted lithostatic. <b>h</b> the area-median
      angle by which the free rake turns away from the tensor&rsquo;s on the
      slipping area at 3&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>. A missing point in b or c
      is a tensor that does not bring half the area to failure by
      6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <b>The first failure says nothing about the tensor.</b> &lambda;<sub>0</sub>
    is 117 to 159&nbsp;MPa and flat in azimuth (panel a): it is set by
    whichever sliver of the supplied mesh happens to be best oriented, and
    it moves with the regime only because the family&rsquo;s intermediate
    stress changes the normal stress on that sliver. The load at which half
    the fault is slipping (b, c) is the number that discriminates.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>Fault-normal compression is the hardest way to load this fault.</b>
    With the compressive axis at 110&deg;, normal to the strike, the thrust
    tensor needs 478&nbsp;MPa of maximum shear to bring half the area to
    failure under either rule, against 387&nbsp;MPa at 130&deg; and
    316&nbsp;MPa at 150&deg;:
    a 60&deg; fault is poorly oriented for pure thrust, and obliquity helps.
    For the strike-slip half of the family the fault-normal case does not
    activate at all: with r&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0.5 at 100&deg; to 120&deg;, a
    third to 85&nbsp;% of the area has its normal stress rising faster than
    its shear and can never fail (e), and half the area is not slipping by
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>. The same tensors at 150&deg; are the
    easiest in the sweep, 186 to 203&nbsp;MPa, two and a half times less than
    fault-normal thrust.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The observed sense of slip picks the oblique thrust-like corner.</b>
    The fault is reverse with a left-lateral component. In this family that
    rake, 45&deg; to 75&deg;, appears only with the compressive axis at
    120&deg; to 150&deg; (d), and near the plate-convergence azimuth of about
    130&deg; only the thrust-like half of the family (r&nbsp;&le;&nbsp;0.5)
    brings half the fault to failure within the scan, at 377 to
    397&nbsp;MPa, with a mean rake of 58&deg; to 73&deg;. The tensors that
    both load the fault and slip it the right way are therefore the oblique,
    thrust-leaning ones, and the pure strike-slip tensor at the convergence
    azimuth clamps half of the fault shut.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The strength profile shifts the numbers, not the ordering.</b> The
    lithostatic model, weak in its shallow rows, has 3 to 45&nbsp;% of the
    area slipping already at the uniform model&rsquo;s first failure and
    reaches half the area at loads up to 20&nbsp;% lower for the thrust-like
    tensors and within 5&nbsp;% for the strike-slip ones (c against b), with
    a smoother activation curve (f); every ranking of azimuth and regime is
    the same. The area the load drives into tension stays under 0.2&nbsp;%
    on the uniform model and under 1.2&nbsp;% on the lithostatic one at
    6&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>, so the linear criterion is not being used
    where it is wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The free rake moves the moment, not the map.</b> Letting the slip
    turn changes the load at which half the fault is active by under
    2&nbsp;% on every tensor (b, c, solid against dashed), switches on a few
    elements and switches none off, and reverses the sense of slip on at
    most a handful of slivers whose neighbours turn their shear round. What
    it changes is the moment (d): by 0.1&nbsp;% for pure thrust at any
    azimuth, and by up to 11&nbsp;% on the uniform model and 15&nbsp;% on
    the lithostatic one at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5 and the oblique azimuths, where
    the regional shear on the fault is smallest
    against what the slipping neighbours add and the tensor itself is
    ambiguous between reverse and sinistral. The slip turns by an area-median
    0.1&deg; to 0.4&deg; for thrust and 1&deg; to 3&deg; for the strike-slip
    and axial tensors at 3&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub> (h), with a 90th
    percentile under 10&deg;. The fixed rake is therefore a sub-percent
    approximation where the tensor is thrust-like and a several-percent one
    where it is not, and the maps under either rule tell the same story.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The kernel does not matter here, and the slivers did not break the
    solve.</b> Under either rule the classical dislocation and the mollified
    kernel put the half-active load within 1.4&nbsp;% of each other on every
    tensor, the active fraction within 0.011 at every load, and the moment
    within 4&nbsp;% at the median. The uniqueness indicator for the
    collocation kernels is the smallest eigenvalue of the symmetric part of
    the area-weighted operator, as a fraction of its median eigenvalue:
    it is positive on all 35 tensors for both, +0.0007 to +0.0009 for the
    mollified kernel and +0.03 to +0.08 for the classical one, the small
    values being the fault&rsquo;s softest mode, slip of the whole
    100&nbsp;km surface at once, against the element-scale median. So the
    uniqueness hypothesis holds here for every kernel, and the projected
    Gauss&ndash;Seidel sweeps converged in all 5,460 solves, scalar and
    cone alike, without the active-set fallback. The slips themselves, tens of metres at three
    times the first failure, are what a static load of several hundred MPa
    produces on a frictional surface with no other faults to share it: read
    the maps for where slip goes, not for how much.
  </p>
  <p>
    <b>The Galerkin kernel draws the same map with less slip, and it is the
    one with a proof.</b> Its area-weighted operator is positive definite
    on all 35 tensors (smallest eigenvalue +0.0004 to +0.0006 of the
    median, the same whole-fault mode), so each of its 1,820 solves has one
    solution by theorem and the sweeps converge by theorem; they did, with
    no fallback. It puts the half-active load within 2.1&nbsp;% of the
    mollified collocation kernel on every tensor (median 0.6&nbsp;% lower)
    and the active fraction within 0.013 at every load, under either rule:
    the same elements slip. Each slips a little less, so the moment is
    7&nbsp;% lower at the median and between 0 and 17&nbsp;% lower across the sweep,
    in the same proportion at every depth, because averaging the
    self-traction over an element reads it higher than the centroid does.
    The same operator is 3&nbsp;% softer than collocation on the
    gate&rsquo;s penny; here, with the supplied 0.12&nbsp;km slivers at the
    same mollification width, the gap is twice that. The map is the
    robust product and the moment the fragile one, on every kernel.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-this-is-not">What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a model of Taiwan: one fault, with none of the others and none
      of the plate interface, so there is no interaction and no shadow.</li>
    <li>Not a slip-rate model: the load is a static stress and the answer a
      static slip, with no time, no geodesy and no geologic rate fitted.</li>
    <li>Not an absolute stress: everything is quoted relative to the
      tensor&rsquo;s own first failure or in MPa of maximum shear, and the
      150&nbsp;MPa and the pore ratio are assumptions, not measurements.</li>
    <li>Not a converged mesh: the segments are used as supplied, at one
      resolution, with their slivers.</li>
    <li>Uniform friction, and no coupling of the normal stress to slip in
      either rule, which is where genuine Coulomb non-uniqueness would
      enter. Opening is not modelled; where the load drives the normal
      stress through zero the linear criterion is wrong and the manifest
      says where.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproducing-it">Reproducing it</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-sh">python studies/active_fractal/selfcheck.py -k taiwan    # the inputs
python studies/active_fractal/selfcheck.py -k rake      # the free-rake solver on a penny
python studies/active_fractal/selfcheck.py -k galerkin  # the symmetric form of the kernel
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py assemble --workers 8                   # the folded matrix, cached
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py assemble --workers 8 --form galerkin   # its Galerkin form, 9 points per element
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py run                    # 70 tensors x 3 kernels x 2 rules x 13 loads
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py figure                 # website/src/figures/taiwan_sweep.png
python studies/active_fractal/taiwan.py payload                # website/public/data/taiwan_*</code></pre>

  <script>
    import { startTaiwan } from "../viz/taiwan";
    startTaiwan(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
